{"packet_id":"D043-01","doc_id":"D043","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"RSO no-fault eviction relocation assistance","requirement":"To evict a tenant for a no-fault reason from an RSO unit, a landlord must file a Declaration of Intent to Evict with LAHD first, serve notice only afterwards, file a copy within 3 days, and pay relocation assistance.","key_value":"Eligible tenant $11,000-$14,400; qualified tenant $23,150-$27,400 per unit (7/1/2026-6/30/2027)","coverage_conditions":"All residential rental units covered by the Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO); all tenant not-at-fault evictions requiring relocation assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"RSO-covered rental units"},"exemptions":"Mom and Pop properties (four or fewer units) and single-family dwellings owned by natural persons may pay reduced amounts; resident-manager replacement, natural-disaster hazardous conditions, and relocation offset against unpaid rent are exempt.","penalty":"Failure to file the Landlord Declaration makes the eviction a violation of the RSO; a tenant may raise non-payment of relocation assistance as an affirmative defense.","effective_date":"2026-07-01","citation":"Los Angeles Rent Stabilization Ordinance (L.A.M.C. §151.00 et seq.)","quoted_span":"All tenant not- at-fault evictions require payment of relocation assistance and the filing of a Declaration of Intent to Evict form with the Los Angeles Housing Department (LAHD) before evicting tenants from units covered by the Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO) or the Just Cause Ordinance (JCO).","interaction":"City RSO relocation amounts must be enhanced for lower income tenants under the state Housing Crisis Act; the higher of state or city amount applies.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D043-01","doc_id":"D043","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"JCO no-fault eviction relocation assistance","requirement":"To evict a tenant for a no-fault reason from a JCO unit, a landlord must file a Declaration of Intent to Evict with LAHD first, serve notice only afterwards, file a copy within 3 days, and pay relocation assistance.","key_value":"Eligible tenant $11,000-$14,400; qualified tenant $23,150-$27,400 per unit (7/1/2026-6/30/2027)","coverage_conditions":"Rental units covered by the Just Cause Ordinance (JCO); all tenant not-at-fault evictions requiring relocation assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"JCO-covered rental units"},"exemptions":"Single-family dwellings owned by a natural person owning no more than four units may pay one month's rent; Mom and Pop four-or-fewer-unit properties may pay reduced amounts; resident-manager replacement and relocation offset are exempt.","penalty":"Failure to file the Landlord Declaration makes the eviction a violation of the JCO; a tenant may raise non-payment of relocation assistance as an affirmative defense.","effective_date":"2026-07-01","citation":"Los Angeles Just Cause Ordinance (L.A.M.C. §165.00 et seq.)","quoted_span":"All tenant not- at-fault evictions require payment of relocation assistance and the filing of a Declaration of Intent to Evict form with the Los Angeles Housing Department (LAHD) before evicting tenants from units covered by the Rent Stabilization Ordinance (RSO) or the Just Cause Ordinance (JCO).","interaction":"City JCO relocation amounts must be enhanced for lower income tenants under the state Housing Crisis Act; the higher of state or city amount applies.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D043-01","doc_id":"D043","jurisdiction":"Los Angeles, CA","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"RPO relocation protections for demolition for new construction","requirement":"An owner demolishing a Protected Unit for new construction must file notice with LAHD, comply with RPO relocation and occupant protections, and pay relocation assistance at the highest of state HCA or city amounts.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Protected Units, including all RSO units, demolished for new construction where the owner files a declaration of intent to evict or notice of intent to withdraw units.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Protected Units demolished for new construction"},"exemptions":"Does not apply where the units demolished are not Protected Units or are not occupied by a lower income tenant; regular RSO or JCO relocation rules then apply.","penalty":"Financial penalties starting at $250,000 per displaced unit and withholding of LAHD permit clearances.","effective_date":"2025-02-11","citation":"Resident Protections Ordinance (Ordinances #188481, #188482)","quoted_span":"Owners who demolish a Protected Unit for the purpose of new construction within 5 years after the filing of the Notice of Intent to Withdraw or Declaration of Intent to Evict with LAHD are required to comply with relocation requirements under the RPO.","interaction":"Owner must pay the highest of applicable state HCA and city relocation amounts; HCA enhancements apply regardless of RPO applicability.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
